{
  "researcherId": "R1015",
  "evidenceId": "SYN-PUB-10523",
  "rationale": "ArmaturaSec d.o.o. seeks to detect unusual behaviour on Modbus and BACnet networks without labelled attack data, as supervised classifiers generate excessive false positives. They state: We need to detect unusual behaviour on Modbus and BACnet networks without labelled attack data — our clients have never been breached (or don't know it), so we have zero examples of malicious traffic and supervised classifiers are useless to us. Vuk Đukić offers relevant expertise in unsupervised anomaly detection for industrial control systems. Specifically, Vuk Đukić has published research on an autoencoder-based neural network framework: The proposed approach leverages autoencoder-based neural networks trained exclusively on benign traffic to establish a baseline behavioural profile of legitimate industrial communications. Anomalies are identified by measuring reconstruction error against learned normal patterns, without requiring labelled attack data. The specific technical mechanism proposed for transfer to the company is the autoencoder architecture trained solely on benign traffic archives to measure reconstruction error for drift detection. Because public dataset training differs from real site traffic dynamics, transferring this mechanism remains a hypothesis until tested on the company archives. The company has expressed r&d capacity: We can dedicate one to two engineers part-time to a collaborative project, with access to our traffic archives, our hardware bench, and our existing firmware codebase under NDA.",
  "facts": [
    {
      "recordId": "C001-CH1",
      "field": "text",
      "quote": "We need to detect unusual behaviour on Modbus and BACnet networks without labelled attack data — our clients have never been breached (or don't know it), so we have zero examples of malicious traffic and supervised classifiers are useless to us."
    },
    {
