{
  "researcherId": "R0976",
  "evidenceId": "SYN-PUB-10216",
  "interpretation": "Jelena Marković is the author of the cited publication on mixed-berry packaging which evaluates polymeric packaging films differing in OTR and CO2TR for MAP of berry trays, uses finite element modelling of gas permeation to confirm experimental headspace trends, and demonstrates reduced mold incidence, directly addressing the challenge of matching OTR to berry respiration and modeling headspace parameters.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "recordId": "R0976",
      "field": "name",
      "quote": "Jelena Marković"
    },
    {
      "recordId": "SYN-PUB-10216",
      "field": "title",
      "quote": "Packaging material permeability screening for MAP of mixed-berry trays"
    },
    {
      "recordId": "SYN-PUB-10216",
      "field": "abstract",
      "quote": "Mixed-berry trays combining raspberry, blueberry, and blackberry are commercially attractive but present packaging engineering challenges because each species has a distinct optimal modified atmosphere range and a different respiration rate at equivalent temperatures. This study evaluated 12 commercially available polymeric packaging films differing in oxygen transmission rate (OTR) and carbon dioxide transmission rate (CO₂TR) for their suitability in MAP of mixed-berry trays stored at 2 °C. Headspace equilibrium gas compositions were measured at 24-hour intervals over eight days and compared against species-specific target ranges determined from prior single-species trials. Microbiological and firmness data were collected at four-day intervals. A film with OTR of 3,800 cm³/m²/day and CO₂TR of 18,000 cm³/m²/day delivered headspace conditions within acceptable ranges for all three species simultaneously and reduced mold incidence by 55% relative to a standard polyethylene control. Finite element modelling of gas permeation through the film was used to confirm experimental headspace trends and to predict equilibrium under alternative tray fill-weight scenarios. The results provide film specification guidance for packers handling mixed-berry formats and demonstrate that a single film solution can satisfy the conflicting atmosphere requirements of co-packaged fruit species."
    },
